in @prism

in @io

# @io/uri
#
# URI decomposition primitives at the sigil layer. The `~uri` typed
# sigil itself lives in boot/02a-io.mirror as a `@sigil("uri")`
# instantiation; its block carries the URI's raw text. This grammar
# is the substrate where that text gets pulled apart into the
# component fields downstream callers — @io/network.connect chief
# among them — actually consume.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-26-portal-as-io-socket-over-content-
# addressed-subspace.md §"open: handshake + frame-negotiate +
# initial frame + stream": `@spectral/portal.open(remote: ~uri, ...)`
# needs to extract the host + port + path from the URI before it can
# call `@io/network.connect(addr: text)`. The primitive that bridges
# `~uri` (sigil with raw text block) and `text` (the "host:port"
# address shape @io/network expects) lives at exactly this altitude:
# above the sigil declaration; below every consumer that wants typed
# URI fields rather than raw text.
#
# Four accessors. Three field projections (host, port, path) plus
# one address compositor (host:port) that matches what
# @io/network.connect wants on the wire. The fourth exists because
# the host:port concatenation IS a substrate concern — formatting
# rules (IPv6 bracket escaping; default-port elision; etc.) are
# small but real, and putting them in the URI grammar keeps every
# consumer agreeing on the same shape.
#
# ── substrate gap closure (2026-05-26) ────────────────────────────
#
# Per the issue #78 plan: this grammar closes gap 1 (URI
# decomposition) of @spectral/portal.open's body. The other two
# gaps (record constructor + autopoietic spawn) close compositionally
# from existing substrate (peer.load's `peer { ... }` literal +
# peer.spawn's `crystallize(...)` autopoietic name) and land as the
# final body-composition tick rather than as new primitives.
#
# Lives under @io by construction so glass_wall (#79) validates
# trivially. The URI grammar itself doesn't touch a syscall — parsing
# is pure text manipulation over the sigil's block. Cross_wall (#80)
# could pull this grammar across the wall once the parsing bodies
# prove halts structurally; the parsing is bounded by URI length, so
# halts is naturally satisfied. Strong cross_wall candidate.
#
# Bootstrap-side dispatch (when @mirror/serve lands): standard URI
# parsing — split on `://` for scheme, then on `/` for path, then on
# `:` for host:port. Default ports per scheme (ws://80, wss://443,
# http://80, https://443, tcp://0 → caller-error). The `url` crate
# is the natural production choice; the bootstrap can wrap it or
# stay on the four-line string-split if scheme support stays narrow.
# No Cargo.toml addition in this commit; the crate pull lands with
# the dispatch landing.
#
# Sibling grammars under @io: @io/network (connect/bind/accept that
# CONSUME these primitives), @io/bytes (the byte layer underneath
# every transport), @io/socket (the duplex handle types
# @io/network produces).

grammar @io/uri {

  # ── host: extract the host component ─────────────────────────────
  #
  # The host part of the authority. For `ws://example.com:8080/path`
  # returns `example.com`. For IPv6 literals like
  # `ws://[::1]:8080/path` returns `::1` (brackets stripped).
  # Returns imperfect because the URI may be malformed (no scheme
  # separator, no authority, etc.); the standard recovery surface
  # carries the diagnostic.
  host(u: ~uri) -> imperfect<text> { \ }

  # ── port: extract the port component ────────────────────────────
  #
  # The numeric port from the authority. Returns the explicit port
  # if present (`:8080` → 8080); otherwise the scheme default
  # (`ws://` → 80, `wss://` → 443, `http://` → 80, `https://` → 443,
  # `tcp://` requires an explicit port). Returns imperfect for
  # malformed URIs, unknown schemes without an explicit port, or
  # non-numeric port values.
  port(u: ~uri) -> imperfect<u64> { \ }

  # ── path: extract the path component ────────────────────────────
  #
  # The path part following the authority. For
  # `ws://example.com:8080/socket?q=1` returns `/socket` (query and
  # fragment stripped). Empty authority-only URIs like `ws://host`
  # return `/` (the canonical default path per RFC 3986 §6.2.3).
  # Returns imperfect for malformed URIs.
  path(u: ~uri) -> imperfect<text> { \ }

  # ── address: host:port composite for @io/network.connect ────────
  #
  # Format the authority as `host:port` text — what
  # @io/network.connect(addr: text) expects on the wire. Composes
  # host + port through the scheme-default-port logic; for IPv6
  # literals the host gets bracketed (`[::1]:8080`) per RFC 3986
  # §3.2.2 so the colon-delimited parse on the receiving end
  # disambiguates the address from the port. Returns imperfect
  # because both host and port may fail to extract; the failure
  # surfaces with the underlying diagnostic.
  address(u: ~uri) -> imperfect<text> { \ }
}

out host
out port
out path
out address
out @io/uri
